  "worked_solution": "Computing ${B}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${B}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${B}^{3}$ quickly, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{3} & ={B}^{2}\\cdot B \\\\  & =O\\cdot B \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n{B}^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
